Original commit body from c526b425de45c1c111675f02cbfd3aff12477eba:

Three vPR-machinery failure modes, all observed live landing PR #144:

1. A non-fast-forward REJECTION (remote branch already held the change
   from auto-pr's own hung-run re-push cycle) was reported 'remote
   unavailable' and queued a duplicate vPR while the real PR sat open.
   The classifier now checks the remote tip: already at local sha ->
   push is done, proceed; genuinely diverged -> loud NOT-queueing error
   (rc 2, handled distinctly at the do_pr and flush call sites).

2. prune judged staleness by sha ancestry only; flush REBUILDS commits,
   so a merged vPR's sha never lands in base and the entry read 'live'
   forever (measured: the sole tree difference was the manifest's
   timestamp header). Staleness now checks content identity with
   .ci/affected-tests.txt excluded: tree-identity vs the base tip, then
   a bounded filtered-patch-id scan (last 100 base commits). Carve-out:
   an EMPTY commit cannot be judged by content (its tree equals whatever
   it sits on) - ancestry remains its only stale signal.

3. An unresolvable sha (branch deleted post-merge) made flush die on
   git's raw 'Needed a single revision' and prune call it live.
   Reconciliation gains a BROKEN class: prune drops it with an explicit
   unverifiable-content warning; flush pre-diagnoses and points at prune.

Test seam AUTO_PR_SIMULATE_GH_PUSH gains reject-same / reject-diverged.
tests/test_autopr_vpr_reconcile.py drives the real script through all
three shapes; 92 tests green across the seven auto-pr suites.

